# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — AetherOps Docker build context filter
#
# The multi-stage Dockerfile only needs two things from the host filesystem:
#   1. pom.xml        — for "mvn dependency:go-offline" in the build stage
#   2. src/           — application source code
#
# Everything else transmitted to the Docker daemon is pure waste: it inflates
# build time, risks leaking secrets, and pollutes the layer cache.  Each
# section below explains *why* those paths are excluded, not just that they
# are.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artifacts
# Excluding target/ is the single most important entry: a full Maven build
# produces hundreds of MiBs of compiled classes, JARs, and test reports that
# are meaningless inside the image builder context.  The build stage always
# runs "mvn package" from scratch inside the container anyway.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
target/
sample-app/target/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version-control metadata
# .git/ contains the full project history and can easily exceed the source
# tree in size on long-lived repos.  It is never referenced by the build.
# Including it also risks embedding commit SHAs and author email addresses
# inside the image layer cache key, busting caches on every commit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IDE & editor project files
# These are developer-workstation artefacts with no meaning inside a container.
# .idea/ in particular can include absolute host paths and per-developer
# run configurations that would never resolve in a container environment.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
*.code-workspace

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation
# Markdown files, the docs/ tree, and demo/ are purely informational and
# add zero value to the runtime image.  Excluding them also means that
# touching a README never invalidates the Docker layer cache.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.md
docs/
demo/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment & secrets
# .env files frequently contain plaintext credentials.  Even if the current
# .env.example has no real secrets, establishing the exclusion as a hard rule
# prevents accidental future inclusion of a real .env file into an image that
# might be pushed to a public registry.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
.env.example
*.env.local

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker Compose files
# Compose files describe multi-container dev/prod orchestration and reference
# environment variables, volume mounts, and port mappings that are irrelevant
# (and potentially confusing) inside a single-container image build.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
docker-compose*.yml

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kubernetes manifests & CI/CD pipeline definitions
# These define deployment topology and pipeline configuration — they are
# consumed by kubectl and GitHub Actions respectively, not by the JVM.
# Keeping them out of the image context also means k8s or workflow changes
# never cause unnecessary image rebuilds.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
kubernetes/
.github/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform-specific deployment configs
# railway.toml, Procfile, etc. are platform shims for PaaS providers.
# They are not part of the application runtime.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
railway.toml

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sample companion app
# sample-app/ has its own independent Dockerfile and build context.
# Including it here would transmit its entire source tree to the daemon every
# time the main application image is built.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
sample-app/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local developer tooling & temporary files
# Test output files, log files, and OS metadata files have no place in an
# image context.  *.tar.gz archives (e.g. milestone backups) are excluded
# explicitly because they can be large and are never needed at build time.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
test-output.txt
*.log
logs/
*.tar.gz
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code project memory (developer tooling, not application code)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/
